# Overview

Momen uses the Stripe Payment Element for one-time payments, recurring payments, and refunds. Set payments up under Action → Payment. The first payment configuration binds an order table. Replace that table only by disabling all payments and activating them again. Binding it creates `payment_record`, `recurring_payment`, and `refund`. Their structure and data cannot be changed.

Create a payment configuration, choose Stripe as the payment channel, and enter merchant information:

| Setting | Required | Input | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Publishable key | Yes | Text | Stripe publishable key |
| Secret key | Yes | Secret | Stripe secret key, bound to a secret |
| Endpoint secret | Yes | Generate and bind | Webhook signing secret generated by Momen |

Bind the Secret key first, then select Generate and bind next to Endpoint secret. Momen creates a Stripe webhook for this configuration’s Notification URL. If the secret key or the notification URL changes, select Regenerate and bind. Do not change the events or the URL of the webhook Momen creates. To handle other Stripe events, create a separate Actionflow with a Webhook Trigger and a dedicated webhook in Stripe.

Under Payment products, select Element. Under Client management, select the web app client. Under Payment result handling, bind an Actionflow to On payment, On refund, On subscription status change, and On recurring billing. One-time payments need only the first two. Refund is not granted by default; grant it only to trusted administrator roles. Publish the configuration before it takes effect in production.

Momen updates the system tables before it runs the bound Actionflow. A repeated notification for the same status does not run the Actionflow again. The Actionflow runs as an administrator. If it fails, Momen does not retry it.

## One-time payment

Create the order in the bound order table before the payment action. Set Payment type to One-Off Payment, select the Stripe configuration, and select Element.

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Order id | Integer | Yes | ID of the order record in the bound order table |
| Currency | Text | Yes | A currency code supported by Stripe, such as `USD` |
| Amount | Decimal | Yes | Amount in the smallest currency unit; `1000` charges USD 10.00 |
| Product name | Text | No | Product name shown at checkout |
| Product description | Text | No | Product description shown at checkout |

On success of the payment action means only that the frontend call returned. Actions that modify data cannot be added there. No payment event runs when a payment is canceled or fails. When the payment succeeds, the payment record changes to `SUCCESSFUL` and Momen runs On payment.

On payment, add an Integer input and bind it to Payment callback → Payment → id. Query `payment_record` where `id` equals that input. Before granting access, check that `grand_total_value` equals the order amount and `currency` equals the order currency. Stripe stores `grand_total_value` in the smallest currency unit. Then update the order. Show the result by querying the order or `payment_record`. Use a subscription query to refresh that status.

## Recurring payment

Set Payment type to Recurring Payment and Type to Initiate. Select the Stripe configuration and Element.

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Order id | Integer | Yes | Order record for the first subscription period |
| Price id | Text | Yes | ID of the Stripe recurring price |
| Product name | Text | No | Product name shown at checkout |
| Product description | Text | No | Product description shown at checkout |

Momen creates a `recurring_payment` in `INCOMPLETE` and a payment record linked to the first order. On payment runs when the first charge succeeds. On subscription status change runs when the subscription becomes active. Read `recurring_payment.status` for whether the subscription is active. The frontend success branch does not grant access.

Subscription status values are `INCOMPLETE`, `INCOMPLETE_EXPIRED`, `TRIALING`, `ACTIVE`, `PAST_DUE`, `UNPAID`, and `CANCELED`. `CANCELED` and `INCOMPLETE_EXPIRED` are final.

To cancel, set Payment type to Recurring Payment and Type to Cancel, and bind the Recurring Payment id. Cancel at period end becomes true immediately. The status becomes `CANCELED` when the period ends, which runs On subscription status change.

On subscription status change, bind an Integer input to Payment callback → Recurring payment → id. Bind a Text input to Previous status when the previous status is needed. Query `recurring_payment` by that id. `ACTIVE` restores access. `PAST_DUE` or `UNPAID` suspends access or reminds the user. `CANCELED` or `INCOMPLETE_EXPIRED` revokes access. Filter the account by `account_id` and the first order by `first_order_id`.

On recurring billing, the Actionflow must run synchronously and output the new order ID as an Integer. Bind an Integer input to Payment callback → Payment → id. Query that payment record, then query the order where `id` equals `payment_record/recurring_payment/first_order_id`. Insert a new order with status pending payment and assign its ID to the output. Momen writes that ID back onto the payment record for the period.

## Refunds

Set Payment type to Refund.

| Parameter | Type | Required | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Payment id | Integer | Yes | ID of the payment record to refund, not the order ID |
| Refund Amount | Decimal | Yes | Amount in the smallest unit of the original currency |

Query `payment_record` by the order ID to obtain the payment ID. Only `SUCCESSFUL` or `PARTIALLY_REFUNDED` payments can be refunded. A refund amount larger than the remaining refundable amount refunds the remaining amount. A new refund record starts as `REFUNDING`. When Stripe reports the result, the record becomes `REFUNDED` or `FAILED` and On refund runs. If Stripe rejects the request outright, the record becomes `FAILED` and the refund action enters On failure.

On refund, bind an Integer input to Payment callback → Refund → id. Query the refund by that id. `REFUNDED` with `amount` equal to `payment_record/grand_total_value` is a full refund. A smaller `REFUNDED` amount is a partial refund. `FAILED` leaves the order unchanged.
